---
import '../styles/global.css';
import HazardTool from '../components/HazardTool.astro';
// Surface the real validation provenance on the page, straight from the fixture.
import baseFixture from '../../validation/fixtures/base_40km_locked.json';
const oracle: string = baseFixture.oracle;
const validated = oracle.startsWith('openquake');
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Fault MFD → Hazard Explorer</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="An interactive tool: see how slip rate, fault geometry, b-value and the magnitude-frequency choice (GR, truncated GR, characteristic, Mmax) reshape a single fault's seismic hazard curve. Engine validated against OpenQuake hazardlib."
    />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com" />
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.gstatic.com" crossorigin />
    <link
      href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Public+Sans:wght@400;500;600;700&family=Roboto+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap"
      rel="stylesheet"
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="gov-banner">
      <strong>Independent research tool</strong> — engine validated against OpenQuake hazardlib in CI.
    </div>

    <!-- MASTHEAD -->
    <header class="masthead">
      <div class="masthead-id">
        <svg class="mark" viewBox="0 0 28 28" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M1 19 L7 19 L9 11 L12 24 L15 6 L18 19 L27 19" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-linecap="round" />
        </svg>
        <div class="masthead-titles">
          <h1>Fault&nbsp;MFD&nbsp;→&nbsp;Hazard</h1>
          <p>How slip rate, geometry &amp; the magnitude–frequency choice reshape a single fault's hazard curve.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <a
        class={`badge ${validated ? 'ok' : 'prov'}`}
        href="https://github.com/gem/oq-engine"
        title="The TypeScript engine is asserted equal to this OpenQuake build in CI"
      >
        <span class="dot"></span>{validated ? `validated vs ${oracle}` : oracle}
      </a>
    </header>

    <main class="app">
      <!-- THE TOOL (hero) -->
      <div class="app-main">
        <HazardTool />
      </div>

      <!-- REFERENCE STRIP -->
      <section class="reference">
        <details open>
          <summary>The four distributions <span class="hint">— all balanced to the same moment budget; only the shape differs</span></summary>
          <div class="defs">
            <div class="def">
              <h3><span class="swatch" style="background:#7c8288"></span>GR — no cap</h3>
              <p>Pure exponential to a fixed M 7.5, ignoring the fault's area limit. A reference line, inadmissible above the area magnitude.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="def">
              <h3><span class="swatch" style="background:#005ea2"></span>Truncated GR</h3>
              <p>Exponential 10<sup>−bM</sup> truncated at M<sub>max</sub>, moment-balanced. The workhorse distributed-seismicity model.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="def">
              <h3><span class="swatch" style="background:#c05600"></span>Characteristic</h3>
              <p>Youngs &amp; Coppersmith (1985): exponential tail plus enhanced rate near M<sub>max</sub>. Concentrates moment at the top.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="def">
              <h3><span class="swatch" style="background:#3e8a4e"></span>M<sub>max</sub></h3>
              <p>One characteristic earthquake carrying the whole budget — the fault as a single repeating large rupture.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details>
          <summary>How to read it <span class="hint">— what the two panels and the markers mean</span></summary>
          <div class="read-grid">
            <p><b>(a) Cumulative recurrence.</b> N(≥M) per year for each MFD. Concentrating moment near M<sub>max</sub> necessarily starves the moderate magnitudes — watch the characteristic and M<sub>max</sub> lines dip below truncated GR in the middle and rise at the top. The dotted line marks M<sub>max</sub>.</p>
            <p><b>(b) Hazard at the site.</b> The annual exceedance rate vs PGA those rates produce, with the 475-yr and 2475-yr return periods marked. Which model gives the highest design motion depends on where the controlling return period crosses each curve — and how close the site sits. Pull the distance in and the large-event models tend to take over at 2475&nbsp;yr.</p>
            <p><b>(c) Where the hazard comes from.</b> Each model's share of exceedances of its own 475-yr (or 2475-yr) motion, by magnitude — a one-fault disaggregation. Under truncated GR the moderate magnitudes do much of the work; under the characteristic models the hazard is bought almost entirely by the big events. Same panel, longer return period: the contribution marches toward M<sub>max</sub>.</p>
            <p><b>Dip &amp; distance.</b> Width is derived from seismogenic thickness and dip, and R<sub>rup</sub> is the closest distance to the dipping plane — so a site on the hanging-wall side sits closer to the same fault than a site at the same distance on the footwall. Slide the site position through zero and watch the asymmetry.</p>
            <p><b>Lock vs segment.</b> Lock M<sub>max</sub> to the chosen area-scaling relation (Wells &amp; Coppersmith 1994, Leonard 2014 SCR, or Thingbaijam et al. 2017), or unlock and cap it lower to impose segmentation. Because the moment budget is fixed, capping the largest event doesn't lower hazard everywhere — it redistributes the same moment into more frequent, similar-sized ruptures. <em>Same moment, more often, not bigger.</em></p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </section>
    </main>

    <!-- FOOTER -->
    <footer class="footer">
      <div class="footer-inner">
        <p>
          <b>Engine.</b> TypeScript, moment-balanced incremental MFDs, log-log hazard integral.
          <b>Validation.</b> {validated ? oracle : 'OpenQuake hazardlib'} golden fixtures + Vitest, in CI.
          <b>GMM.</b> Allen (2012) cratonic SCR + Seyhan &amp; Stewart (2014) site terms, matched
          to OpenQuake's <code>Allen2012_SS14</code> (toy models retained for comparison).
          <b>Out of scope.</b> multi-fault rupture, along-strike rupture floating, full-chain site response.
        </p>
        <p>Type set in Public Sans &amp; Roboto Mono. <a href="https://github.com/james-lagreca/fault-hazard-explorer">Source on GitHub</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
